# Fisch Group

Fisch Group uses 1Password (`Client-FischGroup` vault) with a scoped service
account (`openclaw-fischgroup`). Secrets are accessed via the canonical
`get_secret()` interface -- no direct `op run` invocations in the runtime path.

## Secrets Backend: 1Password

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Backend | `1password` |
| Vault | `Client-FischGroup` |
| Service account | `openclaw-fischgroup` |
| Bootstrap token path | `gbautomation/clients/fisch-group/1password-token` (AWS SM) |

The 1Password service account token is bootstrapped once into AWS Secrets
Manager so that `get_secret()` can self-bootstrap without a hard-coded
credential on disk.

## Usage

```python
from resources.lib.secrets import get_secret

value = get_secret(client='fisch-group', key='quickbooks-token')
# Resolves to: op://Client-FischGroup/quickbooks-token/credential
```

## Bootstrap (one-time setup)

Store the `openclaw-fischgroup` service account token in AWS SM:

```bash
aws secretsmanager create-secret \
  --name gbautomation/clients/fisch-group/1password-token \
  --secret-string "ops_your_service_account_token_here"
```

